id: pin
name: Pin
type: standard
actionCost: 2
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
pin: true
usageLimit: 1/round
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Hold the Character you are Grappling down. A Grapple Check at 1(bT) off, then a
  Might Clash; win both and they are Pinned, lose either and they get away.
text: >
  Pin Maneuver [1/Round]: Pin your Opponents, preventing escape!
  –Maneuver Type: Standard
  –Action Cost: 2 Actions
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: N/A
  –Effect: If you are the Grappler in a Grapple, make a Grapple Check against the
  Grappled with your Dice Score reduced by 1(bT). If you win, make a Might Clash
  against the Grappled. If you win, they gain the Pinned Combat Condition while in
  this Grapple. If you lose either of these Clashes, they escape the Grapple.

  You can only use this Maneuver if you are in a Grapple.

# No target to pick, for the same reason the Launch Maneuver has none: "the
# Grappled" names exactly one Character and the Grapple already knows which.

---

# Two Clashes in a row, and losing either one frees them - which is why this costs
# two Actions and is worth the risk only when the hold is worth more than the
# Grapple.
#
# The first is a Grapple Check like any other: the Grappler is the Initiator, the
# Grappled the Defender, and a tie goes to the Defender. What is unusual is the
# 1(bT) off the Grappler's Dice Score, which is the only time a Grapple Check is
# made at a penalty.
#
# The second is a Might Clash, opened only if the first was won. It carries the
# Grapple with it, so the same settlement that reads a Grapple Check reads this
# too - one place decides what a Clash within a Grapple leaves behind.
#
# "While in this Grapple" is what ties the Condition to the hold rather than to a
# clock: Pinned goes when the Grapple does, wherever the Grapple ends - escaped,
# let go, thrown, or the Encounter over.
